<section class="case-sec" id="problem">
  <div>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A severity-2 investigation is a join the on-call performs in their head at 2am:
        error telemetry in Azure Data Explorer, rollout state in the deployment system, traffic
        history in another tool, prior incidents in a fourth. Each hop is cheap;
        the join is not. Investigations ran <strong>multiple hours</strong>, and the
        expensive part was rarely the fix — it was establishing <em>which of four
        or five plausible stories was actually true</em>.
      </p>
      <p>
        That's a planning-and-evidence problem, which is exactly the shape LLM
        agents are good at — <strong>if</strong> you can trust what they conclude. So the
        system was built backwards from trust: every claim cites a query, every
        run is traced, and a confidence rubric decides what the agent is allowed
        to assert.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="architecture">
  <div>
    <h2>Architecture, as built</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        A hand-rolled <strong>planner / executor loop</strong> on Azure OpenAI — no
        LangChain, no framework. The planner owns the hypothesis register; the
        executor dispatches <strong>10 MCP tools concurrently</strong> against live systems;
        a compaction pass keeps the <strong>200K-token context</strong> from truncating its
        own evidence; and every LLM call is traced to Azure Data Explorer with tool decisions,
        latencies, and retries — the agent is as observable as the systems it
        investigates.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  {% include "partials/diagram-incident.njk" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The framework decision is <a href="#decision">below</a>: the first build sat on LangChain/LangGraph, and debugging through
      five layers of someone else's abstraction was slower than owning the loop.
      Deleting it bought full tracing, 429-storm retry we control, and an agent
      the on-call can actually reason about. The MCP tool layer shipped as
      <strong>four reusable servers</strong> — one almost entirely AI-generated — now serving
      governed query and telemetry tools to Copilot, VS Code, and other internal
      agents.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec decision-sec" id="decision">
  <h2>The decision: fire the framework</h2>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The first build sat on LangChain and LangGraph. Debugging through five layers of someone else's abstraction was slower than owning the loop — so I replaced it with a hand-rolled planner/executor: full LLM tracing, retry under 429 storms, context compaction we control, concurrent tool dispatch.</p>
    <p>The trade-off is more code and less framework. What it bought is faster iteration, traceable failures, and an agent the on-call can actually reason about. Ask me about the day I deleted the agent framework.</p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="trace">
  <div>
    <h2>One run, step by step</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        This is a production investigation replayed: an availability regression —
        elevated 5xx and throttling on one cluster — with two deployments sitting
        suspiciously inside the window. The interesting part is step 06, where the
        agent <strong>kills the obvious story on evidence</strong> instead of pattern-matching
        to it.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="trace" id="traceViewer" tabindex="0" aria-label="Run trace, step by step">
    <div class="cp-head">
      <span>{{ trace_incident.meta.label }}</span>
      <span class="trace-controls">
        <button type="button" data-trace="prev" aria-label="Previous step">‹ prev</button>
        <button type="button" data-trace="next" aria-label="Next step">next ›</button>
        <button type="button" data-trace="all">expand all</button>
      </span>
    </div>
    {%- for s in trace_incident.steps %}
    <details class="trace-step">
      <summary>
        <span class="ts-n">{{ s.n }}</span>
        <span class="ts-actor {{ s.kind }}">{{ s.actor }}</span>
        <span class="ts-title">{{ s.title }}</span>
        <span class="ts-ms">{{ s.ms }}</span>
      </summary>
      <div class="ts-body"><p>{{ s.body | safe }}</p></div>
    </details>
    {%- endfor %}
    <div class="panel-foot">{{ trace_incident.meta.note }}.</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="rubric">
  <div>
    <h2>The confidence rubric</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        The agent is not allowed to sound more certain than its evidence. Every
        finding is scored against a fixed rubric before it reaches the bridge —
        and the score changes what the agent may say.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>confidence rubric — enforced on every finding</span></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Score</th><th>Evidence bar</th><th>What the agent may claim</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
            <td class="num good">HIGH</td>
            <td>≥2 independent tools agree · timelines align · row-count assertions passed</td>
            <td>A probable cause, stated as such, with a recommended mitigation path</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="num">MED</td>
            <td>Single-source evidence, or agreeing sources with a timeline gap</td>
            <td>A hypothesis worth a human's next 10 minutes — never a conclusion</td>
          </tr>
          <tr>
            <td class="num warn">LOW</td>
            <td>Inference without direct data, or tools returned zero rows</td>
            <td>"Insufficient evidence" — plus exactly which query would resolve it</td>
          </tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-foot">a zero-row query is reported as <span class="g">no data</span>, never as "no issues found."</div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="evals">
  <div>
    <h2>The evals that gate every change</h2>
    <div class="prose">
      <p>
        An agent that briefs incident bridges doesn't get to be evaluated on
        vibes. The harness replays a golden set of <strong>historical incidents with
        known root causes</strong> — investigations where the ground truth was
        established the hard way — and scores every prompt, tool, and model
        change before it ships, exactly like CI.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
  <div class="table-panel">
    <div class="cp-head"><span>eval dimensions — replayed-incident golden set</span><span class="g">gates every change</span></div>
    <div class="table-scroll">
      <table class="data-table">
        <thead><tr><th>Dimension</th><th>Question it answers</th><th>Failure it exists to catch</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td class="mono">root-cause hit</td><td>Did the top-ranked hypothesis match the incident's established cause?</td><td>Confident, coherent, wrong</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">citation validity</td><td>Does every numeric claim resolve to a real query ID with matching rows?</td><td>Narratives that outrun their evidence</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">hypothesis discipline</td><td>Were wrong theories killed by a tool result, not silently dropped?</td><td>Anchoring on the first plausible story</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">tool budget</td><td>Did the run stay inside its call and token budgets?</td><td>Runaway loops that cost more than the incident</td></tr>
          <tr><td class="mono">calibration</td><td>Do HIGH-confidence verdicts match ground truth more often than MED?</td><td>A rubric that's theater instead of a control</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <div class="panel-foot">per-dimension scores are internal and stay internal — <span class="g">the gate, not the grade, is the point.</span></div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="changed">
  <div>
    <h2>Results</h2>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>
      The quieter outcome is who acts on it: the agent's brief is the first
      artifact on the bridge, and the on-call validates instead of hunts. During
      a <strong>4-team cross-org severity-2</strong> that same discipline — hypotheses, evidence,
      cited claims — is what let one person run the room.
    </p>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="case-sec" id="lessons">
  <h2>What I got wrong</h2>
  <ul class="lessons">
    <li><span class="ed">2026-05</span><span><strong>The first confidence rubric was miscalibrated</strong> — early HIGH verdicts leaned on two tools that shared an upstream table, which is one source wearing two hats. "Independent" now means independent data paths, and calibration is an eval dimension so it can't silently regress.</span></li>
    <li><span class="ed">2026-03</span><span><strong>I shipped on a framework first.</strong> The LangChain/LangGraph build worked in demos and failed on-call: failures weren't traceable through the abstraction. The hand-rolled loop is more code and less system.</span></li>
  </ul>
</section>

